[
  {
    "number": "1",
    "text": "See:  http://www.bankofengland.co.uk/archive/Documents/historicpubs/qb/2002/qb020402.pdf",
    "source_alias": "nics_fact_sheet",
    "source_title": "National Insurance and the Self-Employed",
    "source_number": "1"
  },
  {
    "number": "2",
    "text": "The average effective tax rate for an employee is calculated on the basis of total labour costs (income tax plus employee and employer NICs divided by salary plus employer NICs).",
    "source_alias": "nics_fact_sheet",
    "source_title": "National Insurance and the Self-Employed",
    "source_number": "2"
  },
  {
    "number": "3",
    "text": "An individual’s existing National Insurance record will be taken into account when their new State Pension is calculated. If they are eligible for more pension under the existing system than the full amount of the new State Pension, they will still get this higher amount when they reach State Pension age.",
    "source_alias": "nics_fact_sheet",
    "source_title": "National Insurance and the Self-Employed",
    "source_number": "3"
  },
  {
    "number": "4",
    "text": "https://beis.dialogue-app.com/matthew-taylor-review/home?sort_order=most_comments",
    "source_alias": "nics_fact_sheet",
    "source_title": "National Insurance and the Self-Employed",
    "source_number": "4"
  },
  {
    "number": "5",
    "text": "This represents the NICs that would be paid if the self-employed were subject to Class 1 (employee and employer) NICs. See: https://www.gov.uk/government/uploads/system/uploads/attachment_data/file/579720/Dec_16_Main_Reliefs_Final.pdf",
    "source_alias": "nics_fact_sheet",
    "source_title": "National Insurance and the Self-Employed",
    "source_number": "5"
  },
  {
    "number": "6",
    "text": "https://www.gov.uk/government/uploads/system/uploads/attachment_data/file/199183/05_ots_small_business_interim_report.pdf",
    "source_alias": "nics_fact_sheet",
    "source_title": "National Insurance and the Self-Employed",
    "source_number": "6"
  },
  {
    "number": "7",
    "text": "The vast majority of those paying Class 4 will see only minimal changes to the way they access contributory benefits.",
    "source_alias": "nics_fact_sheet",
    "source_title": "National Insurance and the Self-Employed",
    "source_number": "7"
  },
  {
    "number": "8",
    "text": "HMT analysis. Rates and thresholds are based on OBR projections for 2018-19 onwards. The government has committed to increase the income tax personal allowance to £12,500 and higher rate threshold to £50,000 by the end of the Parliament. All case studies apply to England, Scotland and Northern Ireland only, as the main rates and thresholds are devolved to Scotland.",
    "source_alias": "nics_fact_sheet",
    "source_title": "National Insurance and the Self-Employed",
    "source_number": "8"
  },
